# ==============================================================================
# .env.example — the environment contract (Practices Playbook Pillar II)
#
# This file lists every variable the system needs, documented, with safe
# placeholder values. The real `.env` is gitignored and machine-local — it
# never leaves this machine and is never committed.
#
# SECRETS ONLY belong in `.env`/`.env.example`. Behavior does not: anything
# that changes what the system *does* (feature flags, environment mode,
# service URLs that differ by environment) belongs in committed, reviewed
# configuration profiles instead. The split is absolute, and it has a
# consequence worth stating plainly: a developer's convenience toggle set in
# a local, unreviewed env file must never be able to reach a deployment,
# because every behavioral difference between environments has to be a
# reviewed line in the repository, not a value someone once exported.
#
# Generate local secrets yourself; never share them, never paste one into
# chat, a ticket, or a commit:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 16
#
# Keep this file in lockstep with reality: the day a new variable appears in
# code, add its entry here in the same change.
# ==============================================================================

# Application secret key. Used to sign sessions/tokens — must be unique per
# environment and never reused between dev and production.
# FILL: generate with `openssl rand -hex 16` and paste the result below.
APP_SECRET_KEY=

# Connection string for the primary datastore. Safe placeholder shown; point
# it at your local dev instance, never at a shared or production database.
# FILL: replace with your datastore's real connection string shape, e.g.
#   postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/app_dev
DATABASE_URL=

# FILL: add every other variable your system needs, documented like the
# entries above — one line of doc comment stating what it's for and any
# constraint, then the variable with a safe placeholder (never a real value).
